#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167695} :activity/id #uuid "af9e80d3-02e1-5428-b1a2-f26d1300e421" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048513 :topic/id #uuid "a5a9450e-27e7-527a-9dc9-ede255310e6c" :topic/math-academy-id 3277} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166583 :question/id #uuid "52f0578f-6ac1-5b02-afa0-3df525bb755a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7956"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6e264d-f845-51a5-acaa-852e5582c9b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522810}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Expected Value of a Uniformly Distributed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "a0d51ad5-eca9-5c25-8c80-53b87f0b66a7" :step/math-academy-id 29664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166584 :question/id #uuid "c562f2c3-f1e0-5484-bbdf-9f178363d407" :question/math-academy-id "e-7957"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4b957bc-c1dd-5f7f-820c-8345a3bded2a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049786}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522783}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Uniformly Distributed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "46b68336-ccb6-54bb-a133-3525ce08a346" :step/math-academy-id 29665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166632 :question/id #uuid "b0e62d58-c20a-5907-8486-eb9f03611b07" :question/math-academy-id "e-8431"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "edde1bb6-42d9-5b8d-a29b-7fa91711f017" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522785}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Uniformly Distributed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "65e0b47c-66a0-5066-9cab-774c93235386" :step/math-academy-id 31405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168086 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed over the interval $[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n![](images/af/af2afd72c74a9d7ca810886490baf28610cb743cbe8c8595158cfc2af702b7fa.svg)\n\nIt can be shown that the expected value and variance of $X$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{a+b}{2} \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result for $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is intuitive as it's simply the midpoint of the interval $[a,b].$ \n\nWe will derive both of these results at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "b5aec157-2481-5e36-90cf-d794324c0d63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5171 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e8ea9dc2-f5e8-5bd0-9c42-9bea7f04ec30" :step/math-academy-id 32475 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168111 :tutorial/content "If $X\\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n![](images/be/be9457e598579e35be37d8fcc86663f4229e6193f02b11c49242d63858406a3b.svg)\n\nWe can compute the formula for $\\textrm E[X]$ using the usual definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}xf(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{x}{b-a}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\cdot {\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}|}_{a}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(b-a)}\\cdot ({b}^{2}-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(b-a)}\\cdot (b-a)(b+a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cancel{(b-a)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(b-a)}(b+a) \\\\  & =\\frac{a+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1bdf6fbe-b1ad-56fa-a9f6-e9f99b79f3d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5374 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Mean"} :step/id #uuid "f3e60acc-a80c-56c2-8829-19a34e9d5154" :step/math-academy-id 33541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168112 :tutorial/content "If $X\\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n![](images/57/579372cf7b7880117ff6e7b4e1f063b29e42f886b06e2277e2dcb1f1240ff11d.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\textrm{Var}[X],$ we use the relation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - (\\textrm{E}[X])^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}{x}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{b-a}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\cdot {\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}|}_{a}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(b-a)}\\cdot ({b}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(b-a)}\\cdot (b-a)({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cancel{(b-a)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(b-a)}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac12(b+a),$ we have\n\n$$\n(\\textrm E[X])^2 = \\dfrac14(b^2+2ab+a^2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-(\\text{E}[X])^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})-\\frac{1}{4}({b}^{2}+2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{12}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})-\\frac{3}{12}({b}^{2}+2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(4{b}^{2}+4ab+4{a}^{2})-\\frac{1}{12}(3{b}^{2}+6ab+3{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}({b}^{2}-2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(b-a)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "16d85ab9-dd16-51a2-97f0-2ca5ef2e3d08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5375 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Variance"} :step/id #uuid "34dfa270-6714-5186-ac3d-804cefaedb27" :step/math-academy-id 33542}] :activity/title "Mean and Variance of the Continuous Uniform Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167040}]}
